WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.005
Welcome back.

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:09.177
This video is about procedures: routes, clearances, separation and revisions.

00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:10.695
Let's start with the routes.

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:13.598
Most transatlantic traffic wants the same few routes,

00:00:13.598 --> 00:00:15.843
because they save the most time and fuel.

00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:19.948
To handle that, Shanwick and Gander publish a set of North Atlantic tracks,

00:00:19.948 --> 00:00:21.371
the NAT tracks, every day.

00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:24.839
Each track is a list of flight levels and a series of waypoints.

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:29.662
It starts at an oceanic entry point and ends at an exit point on the domestic boundaries.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:33.056
In between, the waypoints are given in latitude and longitude,

00:00:33.056 --> 00:00:35.190
usually every ten degrees of longitude.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.875
Tracks operate between flight level two-nine-zero and four-one-zero.

00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:43.461
In the real world the tracks are only valid at certain times.

00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:48.309
Westbound tracks are for aircraft crossing thirty degrees west between 1130 and 1900 Zulu.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:52.241
Eastbound tracks are for aircraft crossing it between 0100 and 0800 Zulu.

00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:56.066
Westbound tracks are named from the north: the most northerly is Alpha.

00:00:56.066 --> 00:00:59.837
Eastbound tracks are named from the south: the most southerly is Zulu.

00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:01.722
The letters I and O are never used.

00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:07.787
Every set of tracks is published in a NAT Track Message with a Track Message Identifier,

00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:08.220
the TMI.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.488
This is just the Julian day number on which it was published.

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:16.150
Check it, because it tells you that you and the pilot are working from the same tracks.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.558
If the message is amended,

00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:21.241
a letter is added, like 001A, and the whole message is issued again.

00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:24.336
Pilots don't have to use the tracks.

00:01:24.336 --> 00:01:27.627
A route that doesn't follow a track all the way is a random route.

00:01:27.627 --> 00:01:30.800
On VATSIM, the latest published tracks can be used at any time,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.664
and not only at the levels published.

00:01:32.664 --> 00:01:36.553
So an eastbound and a westbound set can both be in use, and they can conflict.

00:01:36.553 --> 00:01:39.269
If that happens, coordinate with the other controller,

00:01:39.269 --> 00:01:42.487
and clear aircraft on levels that follow the semi-circular rule.

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:45.672
Two more route types to know.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:51.626
The Tango routes are fixed routes in the south-east of Shanwick: T9 and T16 are southbound,

00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:55.094
T290 and T13 are northbound, and T213 goes both ways.

00:01:55.094 --> 00:01:59.233
Clearance delivery for northbound Tango aircraft must be on HF,

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:01.138
but southbound can be on VHF.

00:02:01.138 --> 00:02:06.921
And the North American Routes are fixed routes used to transition into domestic airspace.

00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:09.391
Oceanic controllers never assign NARs.

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:11.600
They're for your information only.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.522
Every aircraft entering the OCA needs an oceanic clearance.

00:02:16.522 --> 00:02:21.311
Pilots must request it at least thirty minutes before the oceanic entry point.

00:02:21.311 --> 00:02:25.855
They can ask on HF, on VHF if they're in range, or on the natTRAK website.

00:02:25.855 --> 00:02:30.214
At some airfields near the NAT, a clearance is needed before departure.

00:02:30.214 --> 00:02:34.512
The SOP lists them, and VHF coverage is guaranteed at those airfields.

00:02:34.512 --> 00:02:37.922
And when an aircraft enters the Gander or Shanwick OCA,

00:02:37.922 --> 00:02:40.713
it squawks 2000, whatever code it had before.

00:02:42.013 --> 00:02:46.446
The request is called an RCL, and it arrives in your pending clearances table.

00:02:46.446 --> 00:02:49.345
You can filter the table by track or by target OCA.

00:02:49.345 --> 00:02:53.178
The request shows the callsign, destination, track or random route,

00:02:53.178 --> 00:02:57.927
entry fix and time, requested level and Mach, and which station the pilot selected.

00:02:59.227 --> 00:03:01.854
Since December 2024, natTRAK sends the pilot an

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:04.258
automatic acknowledgement of their request.

00:03:04.258 --> 00:03:07.549
That means you only issue a reclearance when there's a real

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:10.449
conflict and you need to change what they asked for.

00:03:10.449 --> 00:03:16.958
When you do, you can change the level and Mach, add an entry time restriction such as pass the fix not before a time,

00:03:16.958 --> 00:03:19.184
or change the track or the random route.

00:03:19.184 --> 00:03:22.476
The conflict detection tool checks for lateral and vertical

00:03:22.476 --> 00:03:25.099
conflicts for ten minutes from the entry point.

00:03:26.399 --> 00:03:29.007
After every check, press Move to Processed List.

00:03:29.007 --> 00:03:33.028
This is what lets the conflict tool see that aircraft when others request.

00:03:33.028 --> 00:03:35.962
Then transmit the reclearance if you changed anything.

00:03:35.962 --> 00:03:40.799
Use Delete only if the pilot has disconnected or entered a wrong request and will refile.

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:46.787
Pilots who are within fifteen minutes of their entry point can't request on the website.

00:03:46.787 --> 00:03:51.032
They ask by voice, and you enter their clearance into natTRAK manually as well,

00:03:51.032 --> 00:03:53.020
so the conflict tool still sees them.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.558
The form needs the callsign, destination, level in the format 360,

00:03:56.558 --> 00:04:01.597
Mach in the format 081, the track or random route, the entry fix and its ETA, and today's TMI.

00:04:02.897 --> 00:04:03.673
Now separation.

00:04:03.673 --> 00:04:05.536
On VATSIM, separation is simplified.

00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:09.663
Between every pair of aircraft in Class A you need at least one form: vertical,

00:04:09.663 --> 00:04:10.916
longitudinal or lateral.

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:12.779
And the numbers depend on equipment.

00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:15.986
If both aircraft are ADS-B equipped, you use the ADS-B minima.

00:04:15.986 --> 00:04:18.366
If either one isn't, you use the other column.

00:04:18.366 --> 00:04:20.350
ADS-B doesn't let you assign headings,

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.953
so the practical tool to stop a late conflict is vertical separation.

00:04:25.253 --> 00:04:26.051
Vertical first.

00:04:26.051 --> 00:04:28.975
Up to flight level two-nine-zero, it's a thousand feet.

00:04:28.975 --> 00:04:36.185
Above it, it's two thousand, except a thousand feet can be used between flight level two-nine-zero and four-one-zero for RVSM aircraft,

00:04:36.185 --> 00:04:41.099
and on VATSIM all aircraft above flight level two-nine-zero are assumed to be RVSM approved.

00:04:41.099 --> 00:04:42.978
In a cruise climb or a level block,

00:04:42.978 --> 00:04:46.523
the aircraft is treated as occupying every level in the clearance.

00:04:47.823 --> 00:04:48.243
Lateral.

00:04:48.243 --> 00:04:54.421
Parallel or non-intersecting tracks are separated if they are at least nineteen nautical miles apart for ADS-B pairs,

00:04:54.421 --> 00:04:56.955
and sixty miles, or one degree, for other pairs.

00:04:56.955 --> 00:05:00.551
For the one-degree rule, there are gentle slope rules that limit how

00:05:00.551 --> 00:05:04.041
much the tracks can change latitude over ten degrees of longitude.

00:05:04.041 --> 00:05:06.980
They matter only when one aircraft isn't ADS-B equipped.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.698
Tracks published in a NAT Track Message that go in the same

00:05:11.698 --> 00:05:14.595
direction are laterally separated for ADS-B pairs.

00:05:14.595 --> 00:05:18.179
Tracks flown under PBCS are closer together, at half a degree,

00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:23.092
and non-ADS-B aircraft on adjacent PBCS tracks aren't considered laterally separated.

00:05:23.092 --> 00:05:26.479
You may also hear about the strategic lateral offset, SLOP.

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:29.694
Pilots may offset up to two miles to the right of track.

00:05:29.694 --> 00:05:32.997
They don't need a clearance, they don't need to tell you,

00:05:32.997 --> 00:05:35.837
and you don't have to consider it for separation.

00:05:37.137 --> 00:05:37.839
Longitudinal.

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:41.755
For aircraft on the same or crossing tracks at less than ninety degrees,

00:05:41.755 --> 00:05:46.758
the minimum for ADS-B pairs is four minutes at the entry point and fifteen miles after that.

00:05:46.758 --> 00:05:51.622
For other pairs it's ten minutes on the same track and fifteen minutes on crossing tracks.

00:05:52.922 --> 00:05:56.417
The ten minutes can be reduced with the Mach number technique,

00:05:56.417 --> 00:05:58.446
if the following aircraft is slower.

00:05:58.446 --> 00:06:01.713
The gap shrinks by a minute for every point-zero-one Mach,

00:06:01.713 --> 00:06:04.360
down to five minutes at point-zero-six or more.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.429
It only matters when one aircraft isn't ADS-B equipped.

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:13.484
On opposite-direction tracks, you can't establish longitudinal separation throughout.

00:06:13.484 --> 00:06:18.127
So establish vertical separation at least fifteen minutes before the aircraft meet.

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:22.813
It's no longer needed once they've passed each other by five miles for ADS-B pairs,

00:06:22.813 --> 00:06:24.281
or ten minutes for others.

00:06:25.581 --> 00:06:26.217
Last, speed.

00:06:26.217 --> 00:06:28.708
Every oceanic clearance includes a Mach number.

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:32.136
After oceanic entry you can tell a pilot to resume normal speed,

00:06:32.136 --> 00:06:33.850
so they fly their economy speed.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:37.614
They must tell you if the speed changes by point-zero-two Mach or more.

00:06:37.614 --> 00:06:42.420
It's best to do this only when the planned longitudinal separation is sixty miles or more,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.717
and you can always give a fixed Mach again.

00:06:46.017 --> 00:06:48.126
In oceanic airspace, any change to level,

00:06:48.126 --> 00:06:52.654
speed or route is a change to the oceanic clearance, so it must be issued as a revision.

00:06:52.654 --> 00:06:55.207
It can be a pilot request or your own instruction.

00:06:55.207 --> 00:06:59.835
A revision can come with a restriction, such as a level to be reached at or before a time,

00:06:59.835 --> 00:07:02.303
or a speed to cross a point at or before a time.

00:07:02.303 --> 00:07:05.622
You'll often ask the pilot to report leaving and report reaching.

00:07:06.922 --> 00:07:08.185
Finally, contingencies.

00:07:08.185 --> 00:07:11.755
A pilot needs a clearance to leave their route, even for weather.

00:07:11.755 --> 00:07:15.675
If they can't get one in time, they'll turn away from the track system,

00:07:15.675 --> 00:07:22.024
broadcast on one two one point five, and use a three-hundred-foot level change when more than five miles off track.

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:24.129
If they can't keep to their clearance,

00:07:24.129 --> 00:07:28.615
they'll turn thirty degrees and fly an offset of five miles, and may squawk 7700.

00:07:28.615 --> 00:07:31.608
Keep the frequency clear, give them what help you can,

00:07:31.608 --> 00:07:34.436
and protect everyone else with vertical separation.

00:07:35.736 --> 00:07:36.988
That's procedures.

00:07:36.988 --> 00:07:41.273
In the final video, we'll cover communications and tools: HF,

00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:44.013
SELCAL, phraseology, CPDLC and vNAAATS.
